# Fox Fleet — Caddy reverse proxy with automatic TLS.
#
# Replace {$DOMAIN} with your domain (e.g. fox.example.com) or set it
# as an environment variable. Caddy obtains and renews TLS certificates
# automatically via Let's Encrypt.
#
# Panel:     https://{$DOMAIN}       → fox-control :9090
# Data API:  https://{$DOMAIN}/v1/*  → data plane  :9091
#
# Instance routing is dynamic — fox-control allocates ports starting at
# 8787. A wildcard block below proxies *.{$DOMAIN} to the matching
# instance port. See the on_demand_tls section for details.

{
	# Uncomment for staging certificates during testing:
	# acme_ca https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
}

# Panel + data plane
{$DOMAIN} {
	# Data plane API (must be before the catch-all reverse_proxy)
	handle /v1/* {
		reverse_proxy localhost:9091
	}

	# Health probe (unauthenticated)
	handle /healthz {
		reverse_proxy localhost:9090
	}

	# Panel (UI + management API)
	handle {
		reverse_proxy localhost:9090
	}
}

# Instance subdomains — *.{$DOMAIN} proxies to the instance's
# allocated port. Fox instances are named by their ID (e.g. "fox-1"),
# and each listens on a unique port.
#
# Dynamic port resolution requires a mapping from subdomain to port.
# Two approaches, pick one:
#
# 1. Static mapping (simple, requires Caddy reload on instance change):
#    Uncomment and duplicate for each instance:
#
#    fox-1.{$DOMAIN} {
#        reverse_proxy localhost:8787
#    }
#    fox-2.{$DOMAIN} {
#        reverse_proxy localhost:8788
#    }
#
# 2. On-demand TLS with API lookup (advanced, fully dynamic):
#    Requires a small sidecar or script that queries the fox-control API
#    and returns the port for a given subdomain. See the Fox Fleet docs
#    for a reference implementation.
